  "notes": "R&B-trap: sung hooks over trap drums, and the research's key sentence is that his method is topline-driven — the chords exist to host a sung hook. So the harmony is the richest thing here (sevenths 0.5, ninths 0.3, `susSubstitutionProb` 0.3 for the sus4 colour the research names, `borrowed.V` 0.25 for the pre-chorus lifts) and the melody is written to be sung: `chordToneBias` 0.82, an arch-weighted contour, `legato`, and 4- and 8-bar phrases. The drums serve the topline rather than the other way round — snappy layered claps, rimshots and finger snaps on the perc lanes, light rolls, and `fullTimeVariantProb` 0.35 for the \"clap on 3 or 2&4 depending on feel\" the research describes. The bass is `independent_riff` with sung-along glides because the R&B-leaning cuts use a real or synth bass rather than an 808. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates this high-medium. ⚠ The 90–100 straight pole is not generated: `session.halfTime` is one flag, and this model is the 130–145 half-time one.",
